    <h2 id="the-problem">The problem</h2>
    <p>
      The four repos should act as one system and stay independently deployable.
      That lives in the contracts between them, not in shared code.
    </p>

    <p class="toc-return"><a href="#toc-heading">Contents</a></p>
    <h2 id="the-decisions">The decisions</h2>

    <div class="decision" id="decision-contracts">
      <p class="tag">Decision &middot; SYS-004 / SYS-006 / SYS-018</p>
      <h3>Decouple with contracts, not shared code</h3>
      <p>The repos talk over frozen HTTP contracts with explicit
        versioning rules. The provider on each seam publishes the shape as a
        committed schema, and every consumer asserts against that one file.</p>
      <p class="tradeoff"><strong>Why:</strong> a renamed field breaks the
        consumer silently at runtime.
        <strong>Tradeoff:</strong> freeze the wire shape and publish it, so
        drift fails a build instead of production.</p>
      <p class="tradeoff">I audited my own decision log in July 2026 and found
        this seam broken. The classifier shipped a third field. The frozen
        contract required a coordinated consumer update. That update never
        happened, and both repos' CI stayed green. Each side's
        &ldquo;contract test&rdquo; asserted against its <em>own</em> copy of
        the shape.</p>

      <!-- ts1-sequence. The sequence behind the verdict at the top of the page.

           .seam-plate draws GREEN / GREEN / BROKEN. It does not draw how the
           two sides reached it, and that sequence is the missing half of this
           card's claim. So this is a MAP: the provider's two steps, the step
           the consumer never took, and the gap where a shared artifact would
           sit.

           The gap is two stubs with a real space between them. An absent
           connection is the claim here, and an absent line cannot be seen.
           Muted and dashed, never oxide: this card is not a reversal.

           No digit (ADR-013). The third field, the July 2026 audit, and the
           green CI stay in the prose above. -->
      <figure class="ts1-fig fig" data-fig="map">
        <span class="fig-label">Map 03</span>
        <svg class="ts1-plate" viewBox="0 0 260 118" role="img"
             aria-label="The classifier adds a field. The same commit updates the provider's own fixture. The consumer stub stays untouched. Neither side can see the other.">
          <title>The provider adds a field and updates its own fixture. The consumer stub stays untouched.</title>
          <defs>
            <marker id="ts1-arrow" viewBox="0 0 10 10" refX="9" refY="5"
                    markerWidth="6" markerHeight="6" orient="auto" aria-hidden="true">
              <path d="M 0 0 L 10 5 L 0 10 z" />
            </marker>
          </defs>

          <text class="ts1-lane" x="2" y="10">PROVIDER</text>
          <rect class="ts1-box" x="2" y="16" width="256" height="30" rx="2" />
          <text class="ts1-title" x="10" y="30">the classifier adds a field</text>
          <text class="ts1-micro" x="10" y="41">a new field on the wire</text>

          <path class="ts1-edge" d="M 60 46 L 60 62" marker-end="url(#ts1-arrow)" aria-hidden="true" />

          <text class="ts1-lane" x="142" y="58">CONSUMER</text>
          <rect class="ts1-box" x="2" y="64" width="116" height="30" rx="2" />
          <text class="ts1-title" x="10" y="78">the same commit</text>
          <text class="ts1-micro" x="10" y="89">updates its fixture</text>

          <rect class="ts1-box-dead" x="142" y="64" width="116" height="30" rx="2" />
          <text class="ts1-title" x="150" y="78">the consumer stub</text>
          <text class="ts1-micro" x="150" y="89">untouched</text>

          <path class="ts1-edge-dead" d="M 118 79 L 124 79" aria-hidden="true" />
          <path class="ts1-edge-dead" d="M 136 79 L 142 79" aria-hidden="true" />

          <text class="ts1-micro" x="130" y="110" text-anchor="middle">neither side can see the other</text>
        </svg>
        <figcaption>
          <span class="fig-what">The provider added a field and updated its
          own fixture in the same commit. Nobody updated the consumer stub.
          Each copy of the shape then moved on its own, and neither side could
          see the other.</span>
          <span class="fig-limit">The map does not show the fix. One shared
          artifact replaced the two copies, and that mechanism stays in the
          prose below. The map holds no verdict either: the CI on both
          repositories stayed green, and that sentence stays above it.</span>
        </figcaption>
      </figure>

      <p class="tradeoff">A real contract test needs a single shared artifact
        that both sides assert against. A change to one copy then fails the
        other's build. I amended
        <a href="https://github.com/sanlee-ys/architecture/blob/main/decisions/SYS-004-classify-http-contract.md">SYS-004</a>
        in the open, and I struck the falsified consequences through rather
        than delete them.</p>

      <p class="tradeoff"><strong>Then I built it.</strong>
        <a href="https://github.com/sanlee-ys/architecture/blob/main/decisions/SYS-018-provider-owned-contract-artifacts.md">SYS-018</a>
        replaced the claim with a mechanism, and it landed on 2026-07-18. The
        classifier generates
        <code>contracts/classify-response.schema.json</code> from its live
        response model and its label constants. It is never hand-edited, so it
        cannot describe a shape the service does not return. The CI step
        <em>Contract artifact is current (SYS-004)</em> fails on a stale file.
        Both consumers,
        <a href="https://github.com/sanlee-ys/kb-agent">kb-agent</a> and
        <a href="https://github.com/sanlee-ys/notes-api">notes-api</a>, fetch
        that published file in their own CI and fail on divergence. notes-api
        publishes the second seam the same way, and kb-agent asserts against
        it in the step <em>Notes read contract matches the provider
        (SYS-006)</em>.</p>

      <p class="tradeoff"><strong>The two contracts are deliberately different
        shapes.</strong> The <code>/classify</code> schema is closed
        (<code>additionalProperties: false</code>). That is what turns an added
        field into a detectable break rather than a silent one, and its absence
        is what let the third field through. The <code>GET /notes</code> schema
        is open, because kb-agent reads a subset of the note and an added
        provider field must not redden its build. There, the failure worth
        catching is a field kb-agent reads that disappears upstream. That one
        is quiet: the consumer parses with <code>.get()</code>, so it keeps
        answering with empty titles instead of raising an error.</p>

      <p class="tradeoff"><strong>What is still not guarded.</strong> A
        consumer that cannot fetch the published schema prints a loud warning
        and exits 0. A GitHub outage must not redden an unrelated build, and
        the price is that a genuine provider outage reads as a pass. The check
        scripts record that trade in their own docstrings rather than hide it.
        <a href="false-green.html">False Green</a> collects six more checks that
        reported confidence they never earned.
        <a href="context-architecture.html">Context Architecture</a> is the same
        split one layer up. There I promote the rules an agent reads from prose
        into hooks.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="decision" id="decision-rightsized" data-outcome="reversal">
      <p class="tag"><span class="sr-only">Reversed. </span>Decision &middot; SYS-005</p>
      <h3>Make the enrichment loop async and idempotent by design</h3>
      <p>A new note triggers a FastAPI <Term id="backgroundtask">BackgroundTask</Term> in notes-api. The task
        calls the classifier. It then writes the returned labels back as
        namespaced tags (<code>category:</code>, <code>domain:</code>,
        <code>region:</code>) with replace semantics. notes-api owns the
        writeback, and the classifier stays a pure provider. I first built this
        loop on Kafka. I cut it, because one user and an idempotent workload do
        not need a broker.</p>
      <p class="tradeoff"><strong>Why:</strong> classification inside a
        BackgroundTask keeps note creation fast, and it decouples note creation
        from classifier latency. Replace semantics make reprocessing converge,
        so it never accumulates duplicate tags.
        <strong>Tradeoff:</strong> each task needs its own retry path and its
        own error handling.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="decision" id="decision-eval">
      <p class="tag">Decision &middot; SYS-002</p>
      <h3>Gate model choice on evidence</h3>
      <p>Default to Sonnet across the system. Escalate to Opus only where an eval
        shows the quality gain pays for itself. The same gate ran the July 2026
        migration to Sonnet 5: eval before and after on the same <Term id="gold-set">gold set</Term>.</p>
      <p class="tradeoff"><strong>Why:</strong> I built the escalation and
        measured it three separate times. I declined it all three times. The
        verdicts sit on the
        <a href="defense-news-classifier.html">classifier</a> and the
        <a href="faithfulness-judge.html">judge</a> pages, where I measured
        them. <strong>Tradeoff:</strong> you have to run the eval to justify
        the tier. You do not default to the biggest model.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="decision">
      <p class="tag">Decision &middot; SYS-001 / ADR-001</p>
      <h3>Record decisions, then make the system legible</h3>
      <p>Cross-repo decisions live as two-tier ADRs. A repo-local choice stays
        local, and a system-level one is <code>SYS-NNN</code> in one place. A
        generated portal pulls every repo's docs into one browsable view that
        deep-links back to the code. It links to each doc where it already
        lives. It does not copy the doc, so there is a single source of
        truth.</p>
      <p class="tradeoff"><strong>Why:</strong> I re-argue a decision that I did
        not write down.</p>
    </div>

    <p class="toc-return"><a href="#toc-heading">Contents</a></p>
    <h2 id="the-outcome">The outcome</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Create a note, and the system classifies and tags it automatically.</strong> The work runs asynchronously, with no coupling between note creation and classifier availability.</li>
      <li><strong>The four repos stay independently deployable.</strong> I claimed a red build found cross-repo drift. It did not. An audit proved that, and SYS-018 closed it. Each provider now publishes one schema generated from its live response model, and every consumer compares its own field set against that schema in CI. One limit stays: a consumer that cannot fetch the schema prints a warning and exits 0, so a real provider outage reads as a pass.</li>
      <li><strong>The whole system is browsable in one portal</strong>, with links straight to the real code.</li>
      <li><strong>A skill map (SYS-007) spells out what the system exercises</strong>: evals, context engineering, <Term id="agent">agents</Term>, and observability. It also names where I am weak, which is security on the agent's tool seam.</li>
    </ul>

    <p class="toc-return"><a href="#toc-heading">Contents</a></p>
